      "notes": [
        "CPU totals include the roles shown. MAX and ULTRA split CPU cores by performance class. Management and real-time roles are TBD.",
        "MAC lanes count arithmetic datapaths. Neural cores and peak INT8 throughput are separate measures."
      ]
    },
    {
      "key": "memory",
      "title": "Memory & Interconnect",
      "metrics": [
        "sram",
        "l1",
        "shared-cache",
        "system-memory",
        "external-memory",
        "bandwidth",
        "interconnect"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "MINI also has 64 KiB of private NPU SRAM. Its SDRAM window gives decoded address space. Board population sets capacity.",
        "STD L1 is for the Linux core and DDR is minimum / preferred. PRO L1 is the per-application-core minimum. Bandwidth targets are peak values."
      ]
    },
    {
      "key": "graphics",
      "title": "Graphics & Media",
      "metrics": [
        "graphics",
        "display",
        "ray-tracing",
        "video-decode",
        "video-encode",
        "prores",
        "formats"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "MINI JPEG processes still images. Each ProRes unit serves both encode and decode. AV1 is decode-only in the MAX and ULTRA targets.",
        "Display combinations depend on resolution, refresh rate and board I/O. MAX targets 2 × 8K60 plus 1 × 5K60. ULTRA targets up to 4 × 8K60."
      ]
    },
    {
      "key": "connectivity",
      "title": "Connectivity & Software",
      "metrics": [
        "usb",
        "expansion",
        "storage",
        "control-io",
        "camera",
        "audio",
        "software",
        "drivers",
        "status"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "TINY camera and I²S use alternate pin configurations. MINI USB requires an external ULPI PHY. Network and display connections depend on the board.",
        "MINI Linux and RT-Thread use separate HP profiles with LP management firmware. MAX and ULTRA host interfaces and software remain subject to definition."
      ]
    }
